toolkit.caveats:
- Chain attribution: chains credit each spike to the largest input of its firing step (the last straw). Earlier subthreshold build-up (steps.v0) is not followed back. Threshold crossings and tick re-anchors count as 'tonic'; firing steps whose inputs were all 0 or below count as 'resid'. This is exact for the hand-built relays, but only approximate for integrating evolved cells, so check the origin shares first.
- CCG expected counts assume independent stationary trains. The moving target makes ratios of 2-6 at every lag, so read sharp and peak_lag: a peak at about 0.75-1.25 is transmission, a peak at 0-0.25 is a common driver.
- Direction conventions differ:
  - arrivals.dir and chain paths give where the sender sits ('M<down' means driven from below);
  - ccg.dir and pathway_weights.travel give the direction the message moved.
  - cells.b (hex coordinate) clashes with knobs.b (bias): join with an rsuffix.
- Positions:
  - spike and stroke positions are exact left limits;
  - receptor rows hold positions at delivery (0.5 after the candidate), while p_signal is computed at the candidate with the world's own formula;
  - tuning occupancy comes from integer-time samples.
  - relabel is geometric and taken at T/2.
- Kernel lesions after t_fork in a body that is still growing also change its later development: s5 still has rare births until t of about 800-1000. Use base_fork='freeze' to isolate the message effect. Paired SEs for GRN bodies stay close to unpaired ones (lives diverge after any change), so use 128 or more kernel lives. The reference lesions on s5 with 8 lives were not conclusive.
- The kernel has no per-cell or per-channel forks (LIFE_ABI section 12 forks are plane- or slot-wide). The nearest kernel substitutes:
  - hidden_off;
  - kill_t/kill_frac (random, not targeted);
  - stuck_t/stuck_a/b/z (forces one site tonic);
  - genome knockouts, which also change development;
  - editing program rows, for hand bodies only.
  The exact tool is the reference Lesion: it blocks only outgoing messages (the sender still fires, pays and twitches), and it is slow (about 4 min for 16 router lives with 2 workers, about 7 min for 24 s5 lives).
- trace_kernel takes masks from the emit bits at birth and has no receptor events or trajectory. In s5 the emit bits never changed, but other knobs (b, g_r, pol, zeta, a0/a1) change about 2,000 times per life.
- pheno_changes skips the continuous knobs asym, sec0 and sec1, which change at nearly every tick.
- Speeds were measured under heavy shared load and vary 2-4x.
- Scratch outputs in the interp directory:
  - pickled s5 traces tr_speck_lmax4_hexprism_sex_s5_{0..3}.pkl (reload with pickle);
  - val_reflex.txt, val_router.txt, val_s5.txt;
  - lesions_ref.txt, lesions_kernel_s5.txt;
  - sig_*.json.
  Nothing was written into the repository.

interp.lateral_role:
In-plane messages are weak, nearly E/I-balanced excitation: ch0 about +1.12 and ch1 about -0.84, net +0.33 (J to rim S +0.6). They are needed for about half of all sensor spikes. Their job is cooperative detection. A sensor at rest needs about +1.65; one receptor event gives +1.25 to +1.4; 1 to 3 recent neighbour spikes supply the rest. A target halo, which spreads events over about 7 sensors, therefore recruits its own detection, while isolated background events mostly do not.

Pure relays (a sensor fired by neighbours with no receptor credit) are 9-21% of sensor spikes and drive 10% of motor spikes. Those relay-driven pulls are the best aimed (cos 0.45), but they are a minority.

Causally, after development:
- kernel lateral_off: -4.65 ± 0.20 (63% of F), all through captures;
- sense-plane only: -2.24 ± 0.42;
- act-plane only: +0.00 ± 0.37;
- both: -4.50 ± 0.53, an interaction of -1.77 ± 0.60, so the act-plane coupling is a redundant backup;
- lateral input into the centre sensor only: -0.20 ± 0.39.

The coupling sets detection gain, not direction. Blocking sense lateral cuts sensor spikes per event by about 40% and pulls by 33-46%, but leaves aim unchanged. Its value depends on the world: the loss is 89/63/45% at lam_s 1/2/4, and lateral_off helps (+4.95) when the target is broader (sigma 1.0). The sheet is only stable because of the downward feedback inhibition: without it, lateral activity spreads and off-target sensor spikes triple.

tests.claim:
Prediction 1, the sensor gate rule on new lives. For rested covered sensors after 512:
- one own event alone fires < 0.10;
- own event plus >=1 lateral arrival fires > 0.75;
- two own events fire > 0.95;
- a single lateral arrival alone fires < 0.01.
